  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and warm conditions expected through\nSaturday.\n\nSurface high pressure remains overhead through Saturday \nresulting in dry conditions and calm winds. Highs will be above \naverage with temperatures in the 60s to even the low 70s this \nafternoon through Friday. Clear skies and light winds overnight \nyield favorable conditions for radiational cooling. Overnight \nlows will drop into the 30s tonight with lows Thursday and \nFriday night staying in the mid 30s to 40s. \n\nWhile remaining overhead, surface high pressure begins shifting\nnorthward on Saturday as a low pressure system develops off the\ncoast of Florida and tracks north. This results in increased\ncloud cover although dry conditions and calm winds persist.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Forecast remains uncertain as a coastal low tracks up \nthe Eastern Seaboard Sunday into Monday. \n\nAn area of low pressure is forecast to develop off the Southeast \nU.S. coast late this week, then track north to northeast along the \ncoast this weekend. The low's initial forward speed is going to be \nslow as it gets trapped underneath a brief, weak upper ridge, then \nquickly picks up speed as it moves northeast with a strong upper \ntrough approaching from the OH Valley. There remains considerable \ndifferences among the global guidance, both run to run and between \nmodels in each run. The ensemble guidance for both the European and \nGFS remain quite dispersive with low tracks well inland across the \nNC/VA Piedmont to well offshore of the Mid-Atlantic. Of particular \ninterest are the Euro and GFS AI models that are more in line with \neach other, both indicating a low track between I-95 and the \ncoastline. The low pressure quickly deepens and rapidly tracks \ntoward southern New England and Nova Scotia Monday into Monday \nnight. \n\nAt this time there remains too much uncertainty to pin down any \nspecific details or impacts to our area. Rain chances Sunday into \nSunday night have steadily increased, especially east of the Blue \nRidge. This is in line with the more westward tracks of the GEFS. \nThe exact track of the low is going to be critical to where the \nheaviest rain falls, though there could be a sharp gradient on the \nwestern side of the precip shield from heavy to very light rain \namounts. It is worth mentioning the higher-end of the ensemble \nspread indicates 2-4\" as a worst-case scenario, but many members \nalso have little to no rain for most of the area - this probably \nspeak the best to how much uncertainty remains. The location of the \nheaviest QPF also varies greatly from along US-15 to the Delmarva. \nContinue to monitor the forecast over the next few days as guidance \nhopefully starts to converge on a solution for this weekend. \n\nA strong Canadian High builds across much of the central to eastern \nCONUS during the early part of next week. This is going to bring us \ndry, brisk, and much colder conditions. Models are indicating the \npotential for another coastal low moving through the region around \nthe Thanksgiving holiday.",
